  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small, qualitative sample of 20 students from two Scandinavian countries, which may reflect specific regional cultural funding and labor structures.",
      "what": "The study analyzes how performing arts students preparing for behind-and-around-the-scenes (BAS) careers negotiate their professional identities and future career paths. Drawing on biographical interviews with 20 BAS students in Sweden and Denmark, the research identifies how students navigate traditional bohemian and entrepreneurial identity types, proposing three alternative identity ideals: the flexible creative, the socially conscious performing artist, and the sustainable artist worker.",
      "why": "It reveals how future cultural workers are actively contesting and redefining traditional artistic identity structures to demand more sustainable and socially conscious labor practices within public institutional theaters."
    }
